-- src/core/dst_driver_plus_compose.ail
--
-- `driver_plus_compose` v1 — the THIRD conformant profile (ADR-001 D5, WI-D27),
-- and the first whose coverage MEDIATES anything.
--
-- Per D10 the name says what is in the system under test: the real traced driver
-- plus `motoko_ext_compose`, whose `on_response_intercept` performs real effects
-- through real `ExtPorts` seams. `driver_plus_no_ops` is named for what its
-- coverage IS; so is this one, and the difference between the two names is the
-- difference the project has been trying to build since WI-D19.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT THIS PROFILE IS FOR: THE GOAL LINE'S CLAUSE 1, MADE A RECORD
-- ============================================================================
--
-- The plan's goal line (decided 2026-08-09, after WI-D23) reads:
--
--     A graded profile that installs compose records a real session and replays
--     it deterministically, and the run's criterion-2 evidence is non-vacuous:
--     extension effects world-mediated, origin-tagged to the performing
--     extension, present in the recorded program and reproduced by its replay.
--     The evidence comes from the discovery and witness instruments over the
--     recorded run — a dynamic fact, not a classifier verdict, so door 3 does
--     not block it.
--
-- This file is the profile RECORD. The run is `scripts/dst/driver_plus_compose_dst.ail`,
-- and the record is worth nothing without it: every criterion-2 entry below
-- names `discovery` as its basis, and `discovery` is an EXISTENTIAL producer
-- (see `recognised_producers` in src/core/dst_profile.ail) whose evidence is one
-- recorded, validated, strictly-replayed session. A profile citing it and not
-- running one would be the fail-open shape `basis` was added to close.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT A READER OF v1 MUST NOT CONCLUDE — four things, each measured
-- ============================================================================
--
-- **1. COMPOSE IS NOT CLASSIFIER-CLEAN, AND THIS PROFILE DOES NOT SAY IT IS.**
-- `make ext_ambient_inventory` at the bound revision reports compose AMBIENT: 8
-- ambient sources and 36 `ExtPorts` field calls in its transitive closure. The
-- 8 are exactly the three classes ADR-001's goal line closes by DISCLOSURE:
--
--     four `std/io.println`     compose.ail:31, author_loop.ail:4,
--                               claimcheck.ail:4, ai_compat.ail:45
--     registration's three      config.ail (fileExists, readFile) and
--                               register.ail (getEnvOr) — see the
--                               `Env`/`FS` entries under D5 field 6
--     one ambient AI            `callStreamResult`, reached only from
--                               `handle_compose_tool`, which this profile
--                               EXCLUDES (see below)
--
-- So the three slot-level barriers STAND for compose at HEAD and `make
-- profile_definition` says so on every run. Nothing here lowers them. What this
-- profile records is a DIFFERENT kind of evidence about a NARROWER question, and
-- the two are not substitutes: classifier 3 asks "what could this extension do,
-- anywhere in its closure"; `discovery` asks "what did this hook actually do,
-- in this run, and did it go through a port". The first is universal and the
-- second existential, and D5's criterion 2 — *"effectful only through D1
-- world-mediated ports, with origin tagged by extension id and explicit world
-- state returned to the host"* — is a claim about effects that were performed.
--
-- **2. ONE COVERED HOOK MEDIATES, NOT SEVEN.** `coverage_substance` computes
-- `world_mediating_hooks = 1`, and that number is the profile's whole claim.
-- Seven hooks are covered; four are criterion-1 no-ops on a declared row, two
-- are criterion-2 entries whose port and origin clauses hold VACUOUSLY because
-- compose binds them to constants, and ONE — `on_response_intercept` — holds all
-- three clauses substantively. This is the first profile in the project for
-- which `coverage_statement` takes its third branch, and the sentence it
-- computes is the honest one:
--
--     extension-model coverage is NON-ZERO: 7 hook(s) covered across 1 installed
--     extension(s), of which 1 mediate the world SUBSTANTIVELY through a D1 port
--     and 2 satisfy criterion 2 vacuously.
--
-- One is a small number and it is not rounded up. It is also the first one this
-- project has ever been able to write.
--
-- **3. `on_tool_handle` IS EXCLUDED, AND THAT EXCLUSION IS THE FIRST NON-EMPTY
-- ONE IN THE PROJECT.** Compose's tool path calls `callStreamResult` — ambient
-- AI — before it reaches anything routed, so there is no branch through
-- `handle_compose_tool` that authors nothing (WI-D26 §5.1, §13.3). A profile
-- that covered it would be claiming criterion 2 over a chain whose first effect
-- is ambient. `on_tool_handle` is the ONE gated slot in the ABI, so excluding it
-- is admissible where excluding any of the other seven would be rejected — and
-- D5's disclosure mechanism, non-vacuous since WI-D14 but never once carrying an
-- excluded id, carries one here. THE CONDITION (S25): the exclusion is honest
-- only while no run under this profile dispatches a tool compose provides.
-- `routing_violation_at` is the runtime check and the acceptance script exercises
-- it; the graded session drives prose steps and dispatches no tool at all.
--
-- **4. REGISTRATION RUNS AMBIENT AND THE PROFILE'S RUNS GRANT `Env` AND `FS`.**
-- Compose's `register_with_config` reads `getEnvOr("MOTOKO_PROFILE_DIR", ".")`
-- (register.ail:9) and then `fileExists`/`readFile` (config.ail), before any hook
-- is dispatched. AILANG capabilities are per PROCESS, so a run that installs
-- compose must grant both, and the withheld-capability poison discipline `make
-- world_state` runs for the driver DOES NOT EXTEND ACROSS COMPOSE'S
-- REGISTRATION. That gap is not closed here and is not closed by the registration-
-- effects amendment either, which is DRAFT and on the maintenance register.
--
-- What carries the determinism claim instead is the RECORD → STRICT REPLAY
-- identity: the graded session is recorded, the program validates, the world is
-- reconstituted from it and the replay produces a byte-identical interaction
-- log. A registration read that varied with the host would not survive that,
-- because the two runs would diverge. See the `Env` and `FS` entries under D5
-- field 6, which state the gap and the substitute instrument by name.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- WHAT THIS PROFILE EARNS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT — per D10
-- ============================================================================
--
-- D10: additional profiles earn coverage SEPARATELY, and this one inherits
-- nothing from either predecessor. It re-earns the four rows that lean on an
-- empty install list (WI-D5's tabulation) and it re-earns them on ground neither
-- predecessor could stand on:
--
--   row 3  driver_only's pass is VACUOUS; driver_plus_no_ops re-earned it over
--          four extensions that perform nothing. Re-earned here over an
--          extension that performs something, with a NON-EMPTY excluded list.
--   row 4  the `extension_effect_fault` waiver. Re-earned, and it is the one row
--          whose GROUND MOVES AGAIN: see `waived_fault_classes` below.
--   row 5  virtual time. Re-earned by measurement: compose has no ambient Clock
--          in any hook path (WI-D26 §13.1) and reads the clock through
--          `ExtPorts.clock_now`.
--   row 7  the `ScratchpadResult` exemption. Re-earned on a THIRD ground:
--          compose provides tools, so `provided_tools: []` is not available to
--          it — the exemption rests on the tool name and on the excluded slot.
--
-- **The other seven rows are NOT claimed.** A row not run is a row not earned.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- THE ATTRIBUTION REFERENCE AND THE ROUTED SET
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Recorded as LITERALS, for the third time and for the reason the first two
-- record them so: D4 makes a table correction re-issue every referring profile,
-- which is only mechanically true if the profile records the content hash and
-- something compares it. Calling `table_identity()` here would make the
-- comparison a tautology.
--
-- **THE PAIR IS NOW A TRIPLE.** Every previous re-issue of this table has cost
-- two profile-version bumps; from this file forward it costs three, and
-- `tools/predicate-anchors/anchors.sh` carries the derived consumer list.
--
-- **Installing compose adds NO reachable core site.** `reachable_core_sites`
-- adds an attributed row only when the row's hook set intersects the install
-- list, and the table's two attributed rows belong to `test_dummy` and
-- `scratchpad`. So the routed-set claim is the same 7/6/1 partition both
-- predecessors compute, and the acceptance script asserts that EQUALITY rather
-- than the numbers.

module src/core/dst_driver_plus_compose

import std/list as List (length)

import src/core/dst_profile (
